Q1. What is a microprocessor?
A1. A single-chip device that processes data: takes inputs, performs computations, and produces outputs.

Q2. What is a CPU?
A2. The core of the microprocessor that executes instructions and runs programs.

Q3. Where are microprocessors used?
A3. In devices like computers, phones, cars, cameras, and embedded systems.

INFORMATION FLOW

Q4. What are the 4 stages of information processing?
A4. Input → Processing → Storage → Output.

Q5. What counts as input?
A5. Sensors, keyboards, microphones, cameras.

Q6. What happens during processing?
A6. Calculations, filtering signals, and data analysis.

Q7. What is storage?
A7. Saving data (hard drives, databases, cloud).

Q8. What is output?
A8. Displays, reports, control signals.

DATA TRANSFER

Q9. What is the basic data transfer model?
A9. Sender (source) → Communication Channel → Receiver (destination).

Q10. What is a communication channel?
A10. The medium that carries data (wires, wireless, etc.).

SIGNALS (THIS IS WHERE PEOPLE GET LOST)

Q11. What is an analog signal?
A11. Continuous in time and amplitude; can take any value and is noise-sensitive.

Q12. Give an example of an analog signal.
A12. Human voice or sound waves in air.

Q13. What is a digital signal?
A13. Discrete values (typically 0 and 1); more robust to noise.

Q14. Why are digital signals preferred?
A14. They are more reliable, easier to store, process, and transmit.

ANALOG → DIGITAL SYSTEM FLOW

Q15. How do real-world signals reach a microprocessor?
A15. Sensor → Signal Conditioning → ADC → Microprocessor → Output.

Q16. What is signal conditioning?
A16. Preparing the signal (amplifying/filtering) before conversion.

Q17. What is an ADC?
A17. Analog-to-Digital Converter that turns continuous signals into digital values.

TIME & WAVE BASICS

Q18. What is a period (T)?
A18. Time for one complete cycle of a wave.

Q19. What is frequency (f)?
A19. Number of cycles per second (Hz).

Q20. What is the relationship between frequency and period?
A20. f = 1 / T.

Q21. What does high frequency mean physically?
A21. Faster repeating signal (shorter cycles).

DIGITAL SIGNAL CONTROL

Q22. What is duty cycle?
A22. Percentage of time a signal stays HIGH during one period.

Q23. Why is duty cycle important?
A23. It controls power/behavior in systems (e.g., brightness, motor speed via PWM).

SYSTEM COMPONENTS (REAL WORLD)

Q24. What is a sensor?
A24. A device that converts physical phenomena into electrical signals.

Q25. What is an actuator?
A25. A device that converts electrical signals into physical action.

Q26. What is the purpose of a microprocessor system?
A26. To interact with the real world by reading inputs, processing data, and controlling outputs.

Q27. What is the full system loop?
A27. Physical world → Sensor → Signal → Processing → Output → Physical effect.
